WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.128 --> 00:00:02.680
(upbeat orchestral music)

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.710
- I think that there's several
reasons to own fossils.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.360
Science is only one of 'em.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.460
- I've made millions of dollars
in the dinosaur business.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.430
- The thrill of discovery

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.530
doesn't get old.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.493
It's almost like an addiction.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.770
- [Narrator] There is a fight going on

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.040
in the world of dinosaur fossils.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.910
The market for these prehistoric
treasures is booming,

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.380
but increasingly, wealthy individuals

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.320
are buying up the best specimens.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.700
And that has paleontologists worried.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.390
- A rare, baby T-Rex
fossil is for sale on eBay.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.880
Some scientists and other critics

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.020
are calling the listing a disgrace.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.920
- [Narrator] Last year,
a baby T-Rex fossil

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.863
was listed on eBay for $2.95 million.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.570
The sale outraged many paleontologists.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:46.530
According to a letter from

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.840
the Society of Vertebrate Paleontology,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.270
if the fossil was sold to a private buyer,

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.490
it would effectively be lost to science

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.920
since private buyers
are under no obligation

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.500
to share their fossils with researchers.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.250
- The scientific community
is trying to hurt us.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.000
They want to discourage anyone

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.810
from making money selling fossils.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:06.643
I tell you what?

00:01:06.643 --> 00:01:08.190
I've been charmed, had a charmed life.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.060
And it's all because I pick fights

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.400
and I'm pickin' fights now.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:13.680
And I'm doin' it in a different way.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.870
I'm goin' after the scientific community.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.297
They need to open up.

00:01:17.297 --> 00:01:19.240
(gentle upbeat music)

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.640
- [Narrator] Alan Dietrich is
the commercial fossil hunter

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.143
selling the baby T-Rex.

00:01:24.493 --> 00:01:26.640
(door opening)

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.010
- This is Son of Sampson,

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:33.610
the smallest Tyrannosaurus
Rex dinosaur in the world.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.720
Son of Sampson was found in 2013,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.770
near Jordan, Montana.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:39.810
- [Narrator] Dietrich's T-Rex

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.300
is a scientifically significant find

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.300
which is why he had hoped to sell it

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.480
to the American Museum of Natural History.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.270
- It's an important specimen.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.820
He has made a big effort

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.580
to try to get it

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.720
into a public institution.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.890
We were interested in it, for instance.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.710
But it just didn't work out.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.400
It purely comes down to an issue of price.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.410
- [Narrator] Dietrich told
The Wall Street Journal

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.020
that he offered to sell the dinosaur

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.450
to the American Museum of Natural History

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:09.800
for one million dollars,

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.500
which was about two million
less than the asking price.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.890
- Usually, like in the art market,

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.830
when it's been on the
market for a long time,

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.090
it's either not that good or
they're asking too much money.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.010
So it gets kinda stale.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:20.843
- [Narrator] But Dietrich

00:02:20.843 --> 00:02:22.860
has been in the fossil
business for decades

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.351
and he's confident that
he'll find a buyer.

00:02:25.351 --> 00:02:26.490
- You know, if you can't wait,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.260
you'll spend too much or
you won't make enough money.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.130
This material's 65 million years old.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.680
I can wait a few months.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.495
I can wait a few years.

00:02:35.495 --> 00:02:38.050
(gentle upbeat music)

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.000
- How lucrative is hunting for fossils?

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.820
That depends upon how lucky you are,

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.200
how skilled you are,

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.500
how much time you have to dedicate to it,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.620
and how much money you
have to invest in it,

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.330
in the first place.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.860
- [Narrator] Although the
commercial market is thriving,

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.950
that doesn't mean it's
easy to make a living

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:56.400
as a fossil hunter.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.320
- I wear many different hats.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.640
So I'm Assistant Professor
at Mayville State University.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.446
I teach biology, ecology.

00:03:02.446 --> 00:03:03.730
(water swishing)

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:04.940
That's the day job.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.990
If I have enough time to come
out here to the Badlands,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.880
I switch hats and become the Indiana Jones

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.036
or the Jurassic Park character.

00:03:12.036 --> 00:03:14.370
- [Narrator] This is one
of the best places on Earth

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:15.910
to find fossils.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.490
Every year, thousands of
fossil hunters flock here

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:19.620
to the Badlands,

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.173
to try their luck at
finding buried treasure.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.640
Recently, one of the lucky
ones was Michael Kjelland,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.540
who runs a small, non-profit
fossil hunting company.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.940
To survey the land,
Kjelland worked out a deal

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:36.000
with the private land
owner to split the sale.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.510
- One day I was out walking

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:39.640
and I went up into this box canyon.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.640
In the distance, there was some
white colored dinosaur bone.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.000
I realized, "Wow, this is a brow horn."

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.860
And that's how we found Skull X.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.900
- [Narrator] Skull X refers
to the fossilized skull

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.510
of an adult triceratops,

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:55.780
which Kjelland is working to excavate.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.820
But digging the skull out of the ground

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.270
is only the very beginning of the process

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.320
of getting the specimen to market.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:03.780
Once the fossil is secure

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.420
in a dried jacket of plaster of Paris,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.420
Kjelland will need to move

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.130
the approximately 500 pound
mound of dirt and rock

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:14.650
across this ravine and then,
up and out of this canyon.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.200
For Kjelland, digging up
fossils is a labor of love.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.350
He knows that he will likely
only break-even on Skull X

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:22.690
but that doesn't matter.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:24.750
His primary interest is science.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.730
- The reason why I decided
to set Fossil Excavators up

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:28.730
as a non-profit?

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.163
Because I'm not in it just for the money.

00:04:31.163 --> 00:04:33.570
(gentle upbeat music)

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:37.430
- I've made millions of dollars
in the dinosaur business.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.850
You know, I love capitalism

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:43.230
because the people that
get to the top of the hill

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.533
get to do what they want
to with their money.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.700
Welcome to my studio

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.683
where I clean and prepare fossils.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.800
I've sold thousands of fossils.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:59.570
Right now, I have a 30-year accumulation

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:04.100
of triceratops dinosaur bones
and T-Rex dinosaur bones

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.990
and Edmontosaurus dinosaur bones,

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:07.340
and some new to science bones,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.980
I don't know what they are.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.090
(loud drilling)

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.120
Like most commercial fossil hunters,

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.490
Dietrich doesn't have a formal
education in paleontology.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.690
But he knows his dinosaurs.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.760
What's more, he's an accomplished artist

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:24.100
who can prepare and
mount his own specimens.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.210
But perhaps most importantly,

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.430
Dietrich has a gift for sales.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:29.920
- Wow!

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:33.140
This is a 20-foot mosasaur from Kansas.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.910
This was 85 million years old,

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:37.450
give or take three million years,

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:39.981
some people say 88 million.

00:05:39.981 --> 00:05:43.010
(blowing loudly)

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.140
This is what you call the
eye of the tiger here.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:46.993
This is what gets kids excited.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:52.420
Buyers, you've gotta
excite their imagination.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.067
And what I like to tell people is,

00:05:54.067 --> 00:05:55.447
"You know, at some point

00:05:55.447 --> 00:05:56.987
"you're gonna be able
to ride this thing off

00:05:56.987 --> 00:05:57.977
"into the sunset.

00:05:57.977 --> 00:05:59.717
"Because your name will live on

00:05:59.717 --> 00:06:01.916
"that you owned this dinosaur."

00:06:01.916 --> 00:06:04.150
(gentle upbeat music)

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.080
- [Narrator] Today, private
ownership of fossils

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.210
can be seen as controversial.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:09.960
But things weren't always this way.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.150
- Commercial fossil hunting has existed

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.720
since the dawn of modern paleontology.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.340
One of our most important
specimens in our own collection

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.720
that's on display here on the fourth floor

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:22.160
is a mummy of a duck-billed dinosaur.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.840
That specimen was commercially purchased

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.920
from a legendary fossil hunter
named Charles Sternberg.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.830
So it's always been part of the game.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.320
- [Narrator] The modern commercial market

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.170
took off in 1997,

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:38.260
when Sue, the largest in-tact
T-Rex skeleton ever found

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:41.393
was auctioned off at
Christie's for $8.3 million.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.300
- Everybody was just astounded

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.490
that that specimen would
get that much money.

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:48.140
I think before that,

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:52.150
nothing had ever been sold
above like $200,000 or so.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.880
- [Narrator] Since then, the
commercial market has boomed,

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.040
driving up prices and making it harder

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.763
for public institutions
like museums to compete.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:02.150
- You know, most of the people in my field

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:03.830
would go for more regulation.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:06.160
I personally would go for less regulation.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.310
Because there's a lot
more fossils out there

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:12.400
that are just being destroyed
by neglect and erosion

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.550
than there are paleontologists

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.113
who can actually collect them.

00:07:16.113 --> 00:07:17.950
(upbeat orchestral music)

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.780
- [Narrator] After days of
careful extraction and plastering

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:23.305
Skull X is finally ready to move.

00:07:23.305 --> 00:07:26.638
(clicking and whirring)

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.870
- With a budget of a couple hundred bucks,

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:30.703
how do you get it out of there?

00:07:30.703 --> 00:07:33.970
You can't go out and rent a helicopter.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:36.090
What we do have is a crew

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.470
that have a lot of creativity.

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:40.390
- We're using these metal pipes

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:41.480
as a bridge.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.200
They're kinda like railroad tracks.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.786
Then we're gonna slide it across here.

00:07:44.786 --> 00:07:46.806
(metal clanging and banging)

00:07:46.806 --> 00:07:49.432
(upbeat orchestral music)

00:07:49.432 --> 00:07:52.349
(squeaking loudly)

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.860
(gentle upbeat music)

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:00.604
- [Mark] I think the
commercial market has a place.

00:08:00.604 --> 00:08:01.530
- Ugh!

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:02.490
- Okay!

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.190
- [Mark] The financial side
of it gives the motivation

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:07.450
for people to go out and look
for them and conserve them.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:08.870
Some of them are just so remote

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:11.001
they might not even be found.

00:08:11.001 --> 00:08:13.750
(metal clanging)

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:15.920
Now the bad side of the market is

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:19.250
people can make up things and
maybe they'll make mistakes

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.496
because they weren't properly trained.

00:08:21.496 --> 00:08:23.647
(engine running)

00:08:23.647 --> 00:08:24.924
- Wahoo, (mumbles)!

00:08:24.924 --> 00:08:26.237
- Yeah!
(loud clapping)

00:08:26.237 --> 00:08:27.070
- Nice, thanks, man.
- Well,

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:27.903
you gotta be pragmatic.

00:08:27.903 --> 00:08:30.320
As long as these things
are collected ethically,

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:32.440
meaning that they're not
stolen off public land

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:33.273
in this country,

00:08:33.273 --> 00:08:36.180
or they're not imported from a country

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:39.450
that doesn't allow the
export of any fossils,

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:40.303
just get real with it.

00:08:40.303 --> 00:08:41.580
I mean, just get used to it.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.480
I mean, this is something
that's gonna happen.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.544
(bang)

00:08:46.544 --> 00:08:50.211
(cheerful orchestral music)

